1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:26,760
Gary, you're in a different spot.

2
00:00:27,520 --> 00:00:30,860
I mean, just slightly, but good morning, everyone.

3
00:00:30,860 --> 00:00:37,360
You know, you give the worship team one little pep talk about how schedules don't matter.

4
00:00:37,360 --> 00:00:40,460
What matters is us spending time in the presence of the Lord.

5
00:00:40,460 --> 00:00:43,740
And now I have 20 minutes to preach a 45-minute sermon.

6
00:00:43,740 --> 00:00:44,680
So here we are.

7
00:00:44,680 --> 00:00:51,240
It's, you know, but what we did this morning was we applied the sermon that I'm about to preach.

8
00:00:52,200 --> 00:01:01,200
Because what we're going to find out, and hopefully a condensed amount of time, is that joy is linked to the presence of God.

9
00:01:02,300 --> 00:01:06,700
And so excited that we kind of already got started on the right foot.

10
00:01:07,780 --> 00:01:14,320
We are in a series on the names of God, and today we are rounding the corner into week four of our Advent series.

11
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:25,280
Week one, Kent went over the name of God, Yahweh, and he kind of shared that the entire Bible is about a knowable God stretching out his hand towards you in relationship.

12
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:32,860
We moved on to Wonderful Counselor, that this name of God teaches us and demonstrates to us how we should live.

13
00:01:32,860 --> 00:01:38,380
And because of that, we can have hope in his wisdom and his counsel, because it will always lead to abundant life.

14
00:01:38,380 --> 00:01:45,440
Last week, we talked about God as everlasting Father who is filled with love towards his children.

15
00:01:45,440 --> 00:01:52,660
And today, we're actually going to pivot from the Isaiah 9 passage and look at the title of Emmanuel, God with us.

16
00:01:52,660 --> 00:01:53,500
So, let's pray.

17
00:01:53,500 --> 00:02:00,060
Father, we love you, and we do just thank you for this extended time in your presence.

18
00:02:00,060 --> 00:02:16,420
We are in a holiday season that is consumer-driven, fast-paced, and often really just distracts us from the whole point of the season,

19
00:02:16,420 --> 00:02:25,940
which is to really just spend four weeks anticipating and preparing to celebrate your first coming as we await your second.

20
00:02:25,940 --> 00:02:32,180
God, that the whole Scripture is about you being knowable and relational.

21
00:02:34,420 --> 00:02:47,780
And God, I pray that today would be just another reminder for us to slow down, to keep our eyes open, to be available to your presence in the midst of the busyness and consumeristic nature of the holiday season.

22
00:02:47,780 --> 00:02:54,980
God, just anchor our hearts in your presence for your glory.

23
00:02:54,980 --> 00:02:56,920
In Jesus' name I pray.

24
00:02:57,800 --> 00:02:58,200
Amen.

25
00:02:58,200 --> 00:03:05,760
Well, I don't know about you, but with the change of seasons has come an uptick in my trips to my storage shed.

26
00:03:05,760 --> 00:03:09,660
This is an actual tub from my storage shed.

27
00:03:09,660 --> 00:03:16,380
We've been taking out summer clothes, bringing in winter clothes, taking out fall decorations, bringing in Christmas decorations.

28
00:03:16,380 --> 00:03:23,800
And what I have become increasingly aware of over the past few weeks is that the labels on my storage bins are practically useless.

29
00:03:23,800 --> 00:03:27,560
Anybody else, or am I the only one?

30
00:03:27,560 --> 00:03:32,960
Now, I do want to make a distinction that there are storage bins that my wife is in charge of.

31
00:03:32,960 --> 00:03:37,000
Those are labeled correctly, and what is on the lid is inside the bin.

32
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:44,120
But any bin that I have had my hands on in the past few years often has, like, three different things written on it.

33
00:03:44,260 --> 00:03:46,900
Some are scratched out, some are scratched out partially.

34
00:03:46,900 --> 00:03:55,160
And what's really interesting is when you open it up, and it's actually something that is in none of the three labels on the tub.

35
00:03:55,160 --> 00:04:02,240
And I actually, I found my hunting clothes the other day in the tub labeled fall decor.

36
00:04:05,820 --> 00:04:15,240
Which, if you know my track record for hunting, it kind of is fall decor, because in order for them to be hunting clothes, I would have to come home with a deer.

37
00:04:15,240 --> 00:04:16,860
And so, you know, here we are.

38
00:04:16,860 --> 00:04:21,480
But it makes finding what I'm looking for very difficult.

39
00:04:22,460 --> 00:04:33,360
And the past few weeks, as I have kind of been on a rabbit trail, and I've got to admit to you up front that I need you guys to do your best to follow along with me, because I have been on a rabbit trail in study.

40
00:04:33,360 --> 00:04:36,660
And this message is a result of that rabbit trail.

41
00:04:36,920 --> 00:04:46,360
And by the end of it, you can tell that it's a rabbit trail, which I usually try and consolidate, organize, and present to you a much more polished version.

42
00:04:46,360 --> 00:04:49,900
But I'm not, we're on a rabbit trail together, okay?

43
00:04:49,900 --> 00:04:51,220
So I need you to focus with me.

44
00:04:51,280 --> 00:05:00,260
But as I've been on this rabbit trail, I've realized that I think joy is one of these biblical words where oftentimes we have containers, we have this container of joy.

45
00:05:00,260 --> 00:05:08,620
And we've got a few different labels on the top, sermons that we've heard about joy, some, like, working definitions of what joy is.

46
00:05:08,620 --> 00:05:10,000
And I have this myself.

47
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:12,300
I've preached on joy many times in the past.

48
00:05:12,700 --> 00:05:32,740
And yet this week, these actually past three weeks, as I've been on this rabbit trail, I'm starting to open up this tub and realize that what's inside the biblical idea of joy is actually a little bit different and more robust and actually kind of slightly disconnected from the label that I have on the lid.

49
00:05:32,740 --> 00:05:34,060
Does that make sense?

50
00:05:34,060 --> 00:05:41,400
And so what I want to do this morning is, and maybe you guys nailed it, maybe you guys know your label for joy is perfect and I'm learning new things.

51
00:05:41,400 --> 00:05:50,220
But what I want to do is help you kind of follow this rabbit trail that I've been on that has given me a more clear understanding of what biblical joy is.

52
00:05:50,220 --> 00:05:57,380
Because I think the more we understand what it is, the better we can walk in it, experience it in real and meaningful ways.

53
00:05:57,380 --> 00:06:03,200
And so my goal today is going to be for you to consider me your Marie Kondo.

54
00:06:03,200 --> 00:06:05,480
Anybody?

55
00:06:05,480 --> 00:06:06,000
Anybody?

56
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:10,920
I knew that joke was going to land for like 12 people, but I wanted to do it anyway.

57
00:06:12,020 --> 00:06:17,080
So we're going to tidy up our definition of joy this morning.

58
00:06:17,080 --> 00:06:18,480
I'm so proud of myself for that one.

59
00:06:18,480 --> 00:06:18,780
All right.

60
00:06:18,780 --> 00:06:21,660
Just ask your neighbor that laughed and it'll be fine.

61
00:06:21,660 --> 00:06:26,260
So a little bit of backstory before we really chase down the rabbit trail.

62
00:06:28,740 --> 00:06:35,060
A few weeks ago, I leaned into this topic of psychology called attachment theory.

63
00:06:35,060 --> 00:06:42,560
And as I kind of went down this rabbit hole, I got to acknowledge that Marissa has been on the same rabbit hole, rabbit trail.

64
00:06:43,200 --> 00:06:44,480
Why do I keep saying rabbit hole?

65
00:06:44,480 --> 00:06:46,580
All right.

66
00:06:46,580 --> 00:06:47,400
Rabbit trail.

67
00:06:47,400 --> 00:06:48,460
Are we good?

68
00:06:48,460 --> 00:06:50,780
I feel like I'm going to pass out.

69
00:06:50,780 --> 00:06:51,080
All right.

70
00:06:51,080 --> 00:06:58,940
Marissa, my wife has been on this rabbit trail for like a year prior to this, but we'd never had any conversations about it.

71
00:06:58,940 --> 00:07:01,280
And so I come home and I'm like, this thing I learned.

72
00:07:01,280 --> 00:07:04,820
And she's like, oh yeah, well, here's seven other things that I learned about that same thing.

73
00:07:04,820 --> 00:07:07,140
And I was like, okay, that's my sermon.

74
00:07:07,140 --> 00:07:11,380
And so I've been following a rabbit trail that Marissa has been on for about a year.

75
00:07:11,380 --> 00:07:12,780
So I want to give her some credit for that.

76
00:07:12,780 --> 00:07:15,060
But I leaned into this topic of attachment theory.

77
00:07:15,060 --> 00:07:17,100
It's a psychology idea.

78
00:07:17,100 --> 00:07:21,920
And I actually started leaning into it when I was studying the title of Everlasting Father.

79
00:07:21,920 --> 00:07:30,740
Because attachment theory is all about how your kind of first years of life, what was your relationship like with your primary caregiver?

80
00:07:31,180 --> 00:07:38,220
And how that actually forms kind of neurological pathways in our brain that actually affect how we do relationships for the rest of our lives.

81
00:07:38,220 --> 00:07:44,280
And so any of you who have been in therapy have probably interacted with some of these topics before.

82
00:07:44,280 --> 00:07:45,680
But I was like, man, what is it?

83
00:07:45,680 --> 00:07:53,480
What might our formative years with our primary caregivers, how might that impact the way that we think about God as Everlasting Father?

84
00:07:54,080 --> 00:08:02,360
And so I was on this kind of journey trying to figure out how that might work into my sermon last week on love and Everlasting Father.

85
00:08:02,360 --> 00:08:08,140
And it turned out that joy just kept coming up over and over and over again in this idea of attachment theory.

86
00:08:08,140 --> 00:08:15,040
And I was like, huh, I don't, those seem disconnected to me.

87
00:08:15,040 --> 00:08:22,280
So then as I was studying attachment theory, that led me into the field of neuroscience, which I have no business being in the field of neuroscience.

88
00:08:24,080 --> 00:08:35,300
But as I interacted with some neuroscience ideas, it just kept coming up, this topic of joy being connected to your relationship with your primary caregiver.

89
00:08:35,300 --> 00:08:40,860
And the illustration they use is like when you watch a parent interact with their baby.

90
00:08:40,860 --> 00:08:48,480
And making eye contact, this facial connection, and the baby begins to smile, and the mom or the dad begins to smile.

91
00:08:48,480 --> 00:08:53,380
And there's this mutual reciprocal joy that is rooted in a relationship of delight.

92
00:08:53,380 --> 00:09:03,240
And the neuroscientists actually call this kind of like this, they label it as this joy section of your brain that begins to develop before you even learn language.

93
00:09:03,240 --> 00:09:07,640
It's literally based on this face-to-face relationship with your primary caregiver.

94
00:09:07,640 --> 00:09:16,180
Jacques Poncep, who is the father of effective neuroscience, I know you guys all are familiar with him.

95
00:09:17,200 --> 00:09:22,220
He labeled the brain into seven primary emotional systems, and one of those is joy.

96
00:09:22,220 --> 00:09:29,340
It's this idea of this face-to-face interaction of mutual kind of delight and relationship.

97
00:09:29,340 --> 00:09:43,980
And so as I got down this rabbit trail further and further, I came across this neuroscientist who defined joy ultimately in the idea of safe and secure relationships.

98
00:09:44,620 --> 00:09:50,400
And I don't know about you, but that like is definitely sideways from what I think about when I think about joy.

99
00:09:50,400 --> 00:09:55,720
It's not really what I have on the top of the label on my joy container.

100
00:09:55,720 --> 00:10:00,540
And so I was like, hmm, I'm a little bit skeptical, but I kept chasing this rabbit trail down.

101
00:10:00,540 --> 00:10:07,860
And I found one neuroscientist say this way, joy emerges when the nervous system detects safety in another's face and voice.

102
00:10:09,160 --> 00:10:13,080
And I was like, again, not really what I think about when I think about joy.

103
00:10:13,080 --> 00:10:20,480
I've preached on joy in the past, and this doesn't really look like anything, any one of my previous working definitions.

104
00:10:20,480 --> 00:10:21,840
So I was a little bit skeptical.

105
00:10:21,840 --> 00:10:27,640
And so I went from attachment theory to neuroscience, back to attachment theory.

106
00:10:27,640 --> 00:10:29,760
You guys following with me?

107
00:10:29,760 --> 00:10:31,100
I know it's hard.

108
00:10:31,100 --> 00:10:35,280
I promise I will make it make sense, at least in my brain.

109
00:10:35,280 --> 00:10:44,420
I went back to attachment theory, but this time with some Christian, with a Christian neuroscientist and psychiatrist who specialize in attachment theory.

110
00:10:44,580 --> 00:10:46,500
And this is their definition of joy.

111
00:10:46,500 --> 00:10:49,020
These are Christians who know the Bible, theologians.

112
00:10:49,020 --> 00:10:54,220
They say joy is the experience of being with someone who is glad to be with you.

113
00:10:54,220 --> 00:10:59,000
And apparently some of you have already known this for a long time.

114
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:01,960
I'm so thankful that I'm learning new things with you in front of you.

115
00:11:01,960 --> 00:11:04,300
But again, like I read this definition.

116
00:11:04,300 --> 00:11:09,900
I'm like, this is really good, but it's not how I would have defined joy previously.

117
00:11:10,840 --> 00:11:17,880
And so at this point, chasing the rabbit trail, both secular and Christian psychologists and neuroscientists were all saying very similar things about joy.

118
00:11:17,880 --> 00:11:21,420
That looked a lot different than my working definition.

119
00:11:21,420 --> 00:11:23,920
So I did what any good pastor would do.

120
00:11:23,920 --> 00:11:26,440
I did a word study on joy in the Scriptures.

121
00:11:26,440 --> 00:11:39,000
And I was immediately blown away by the amount of times there is a clear and obvious connection between joy and face-to-face presence in safe relationships in the Scriptures.

122
00:11:39,800 --> 00:11:41,320
A couple examples here.

123
00:11:41,320 --> 00:11:43,260
Psalm chapter 16, verse 11.

124
00:11:43,260 --> 00:11:48,540
And I could have done a 40-minute section in my sermon just on examples.

125
00:11:48,540 --> 00:11:50,120
So like we're just going to fly through these.

126
00:11:50,120 --> 00:11:51,700
Psalm 16, verse 11.

127
00:11:51,700 --> 00:11:53,560
You make known to me the path of life.

128
00:11:53,560 --> 00:12:00,160
You will fill me with joy in your presence, with eternal pleasures at your right hand.

129
00:12:00,160 --> 00:12:01,140
Psalm 21, 6.

130
00:12:01,140 --> 00:12:08,960
Surely you have granted him unending blessings and made him glad with the joy of your presence.

131
00:12:09,760 --> 00:12:23,380
John 15, which is Jesus' most famous talk about abiding in the presence of God, in like presence relationship about the vine and the branch and abiding.

132
00:12:23,380 --> 00:12:25,360
He says this in John 15.

133
00:12:25,360 --> 00:12:30,840
If you keep my commandments, you will remain in my love, just as I have kept my Father's commandments and remain in his love.

134
00:12:30,840 --> 00:12:32,200
I have told you this.

135
00:12:32,200 --> 00:12:41,540
This idea of abiding, being in the presence of, in relationship with, I've told you this so that my joy may be in you and that your joy may be complete.

136
00:12:42,220 --> 00:12:46,760
The last one that really blew me away, 2 John 1, or chapter 1, verse 12.

137
00:12:46,760 --> 00:12:49,900
John writes to a church, an early church, and he says,

138
00:12:49,900 --> 00:12:54,100
I have much to write to you, but I do not want to use paper and ink.

139
00:12:54,100 --> 00:13:02,660
Instead, I hope to visit you and talk with you face to face so that our joy may be complete.

140
00:13:04,220 --> 00:13:18,380
So when I began to put all of this together, it seems to me like there is a clear and accurate definition of biblical joy that emphasizes this priority on face to face relationships with mutual delight.

141
00:13:18,380 --> 00:13:23,120
That there's security in relationship that leads to joy.

142
00:13:23,120 --> 00:13:30,580
And I had never really made this direct connection that joy really required the presence of another person.

143
00:13:30,580 --> 00:13:37,400
And typically, what we're going to get to, obviously, is that it requires the presence of God to have full, everlasting joy.

144
00:13:37,400 --> 00:13:41,980
But I never really thought about the idea that joy was rooted in relationship.

145
00:13:41,980 --> 00:13:45,140
Am I the only one that is learning new things today?

146
00:13:45,140 --> 00:13:46,980
Okay, thank you.

147
00:13:46,980 --> 00:13:47,480
That's good.

148
00:13:47,720 --> 00:13:50,080
I was nervous because, you know, I don't know everything.

149
00:13:50,080 --> 00:13:54,320
And then one scholar said it, put it this way.

150
00:13:54,320 --> 00:14:01,780
They said, pleasure can be experienced alone, but joy always requires another person.

151
00:14:01,780 --> 00:14:03,340
Pleasure.

152
00:14:03,340 --> 00:14:08,480
These are similar emotions that oftentimes we get different emotions confused.

153
00:14:08,480 --> 00:14:10,340
Complex emotions get blended together.

154
00:14:10,340 --> 00:14:17,020
He says, pleasure can be experienced alone, but joy always requires another person.

155
00:14:17,060 --> 00:14:23,660
Which leads me to our Scriptures for the day.

156
00:14:23,660 --> 00:14:31,800
One was read to us by the flock hearts this morning in Luke chapter 2, verse 10, where the angel comes to the shepherds and says,

157
00:14:31,940 --> 00:14:38,540
Do not be afraid, I bring you good news that will cause great joy for all people.

158
00:14:38,540 --> 00:14:52,320
And the question then for me becomes, how can the birth of an oppressed and impoverished infant in the one-stop light town of the Podunk Hills of the Judean countryside 2,000 years ago,

159
00:14:52,320 --> 00:14:56,860
possibly bring good news that will lead to great joy for all people.

160
00:14:56,860 --> 00:14:58,860
Right?

161
00:14:56,860 --> 00:14:58,920
This is what the angel is announcing.

162
00:14:58,920 --> 00:15:05,320
The birth of this infant who is oppressed and impoverished in a podunk town in the middle of nowhere.

163
00:15:05,320 --> 00:15:11,800
How can the birth of this one individual provide good news that will lead to great joy for all people?

164
00:15:11,800 --> 00:15:20,020
It sounds like a ridiculous statement unless you understand that joy is the emotional response of a safe and secure relationship with mutual delight.

165
00:15:21,820 --> 00:15:26,680
Which leads us to Matthew's account of the story when he quotes the prophet Isaiah saying,

166
00:15:26,680 --> 00:15:35,060
The virgin will conceive and give birth to a son, and they will call him Emmanuel, which means God with us.

167
00:15:36,380 --> 00:15:39,860
This is where we begin to make the connections work.

168
00:15:39,860 --> 00:15:46,080
If our working definition of joy is correct, then true and everlasting joy is now available to each one of us

169
00:15:46,080 --> 00:15:52,660
because this oppressed, impoverished infant in a one-stop light town in the Podunk Hills of the Judean countryside was born

170
00:15:52,660 --> 00:15:56,880
and because that infant is not just an infant, but he is God in the flesh.

171
00:15:56,880 --> 00:16:04,760
True and everlasting joy is now available to us because the everlasting Father who created us

172
00:16:04,760 --> 00:16:07,020
and sustains us comes near to us.

173
00:16:07,020 --> 00:16:17,820
Emmanuel means that God delights in you, and he is seeking out a face-to-face relationship of delight resulting in joy.

174
00:16:17,820 --> 00:16:20,720
This is where joy comes from.

175
00:16:20,720 --> 00:16:27,720
God loves you, that you are safe and secure and delighted, and the same way that you watch a mother

176
00:16:27,720 --> 00:16:32,920
dote over their new infant baby, the Lord has come to dwell in our presence.

177
00:16:33,080 --> 00:16:36,580
He has come for a face-to-face, safe, secure relationship of delight.

178
00:16:37,720 --> 00:16:46,380
He delights in you so much so that he came to be with us and to die in our place.

179
00:16:46,380 --> 00:16:49,380
This is what we were made for.

180
00:16:49,380 --> 00:16:50,680
This is what our heart aches for.

181
00:16:50,680 --> 00:16:58,280
Think about the early, the first pages of Scripture where you hear that the God who made us was walking in the cool of the garden,

182
00:16:58,280 --> 00:16:59,600
looking for Adam and Eve.

183
00:17:00,300 --> 00:17:06,800
We were created for this face-to-face relationship that produces ultimate joy, joy that can't be shaken,

184
00:17:06,800 --> 00:17:09,920
and we have turned away from that relationship.

185
00:17:09,920 --> 00:17:15,640
And therefore, we are all depleted in some way, shape, or form of the joy that we were made for.

186
00:17:17,020 --> 00:17:20,660
And we all go searching for it in different places and in different ways.

187
00:17:22,840 --> 00:17:30,240
And we can't find it apart from the everlasting Father, the Prince of Peace, Emmanuel, who is God with us.

188
00:17:30,240 --> 00:17:36,280
My first point for this morning is that Emmanuel is the source of everlasting joy.

189
00:17:37,560 --> 00:17:44,060
We read about, in the Scriptures, the triune nature of God, the God, the Father, God, the Son, and God, the Holy Spirit.

190
00:17:44,060 --> 00:17:48,420
We're in relationship with one another before creation even began.

191
00:17:48,420 --> 00:17:51,620
It is out of joy that they even create us.

192
00:17:51,620 --> 00:17:55,500
They didn't need to create us.

193
00:17:55,500 --> 00:17:58,920
They created us out of their joyful relationship with one another.

194
00:17:58,920 --> 00:18:03,020
In his presence is fullness of joy.

195
00:18:06,940 --> 00:18:12,540
We've disconnected from this secure relationship, and one of the consequences of that decision is we have depleted joy,

196
00:18:12,540 --> 00:18:18,520
which wreaks all kinds of havoc in our lives and in our culture and the world around us.

197
00:18:18,520 --> 00:18:22,200
We're searching for stability and connection in all the wrong places.

198
00:18:22,200 --> 00:18:24,640
We feel like we're adrift to sea without an anchor.

199
00:18:24,640 --> 00:18:27,620
Just look around our culture.

200
00:18:27,620 --> 00:18:29,940
Think about your life.

201
00:18:29,940 --> 00:18:36,300
We're all just little babies looking for someone to tell us that we're safe, that we're loved,

202
00:18:36,380 --> 00:18:37,400
that it's going to be okay.

203
00:18:37,400 --> 00:18:40,540
And this is the title of Emmanuel.

204
00:18:40,540 --> 00:18:46,700
This is what it brings to us, that Jesus became God in the flesh to dwell among us,

205
00:18:46,700 --> 00:18:51,880
to demonstrate to us that we are safe and secure and that everything's going to be okay

